From: baseband lane
Subject: 0532 pandora_pas: the read-back must also check the zeroed padding

0531 compares each segment's file bytes against what ended up in the carveout.  The bytes after them,
up to p_memsz, are zeroed by the same loop and are just as much part of the image the secure world
checks: if that write did not land, the image's interior is not what its segments claim while every
file byte still matches.

So the same pass now also requires the padding to read back as zero, and the printed byte count
covers the padding too.  On this phone's two images the padding is small, so the cast is negligible.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/pandora_pas.c b/b/bsd/dev/pandora_pas.c
index 477ba70..b66cdc2 100755
--- a/bsd/dev/pandora_pas.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/pandora_pas.c
@@ -416,6 +416,14 @@ pandora_pas_load(const char *prefix, const char *base, unsigned pas, uint64_t me
 					}
 				}
 				checked += ph[i].p_filesz;
+				/* The padding was zeroed above; if that write did not land, the secure
+				 * world gets an image whose interior is not what the segment claims. */
+				for (uint32_t k = ph[i].p_filesz; k < ph[i].p_memsz; k++) {
+					if (at[k]) {
+						differ++;
+					}
+				}
+				checked += ph[i].p_memsz - ph[i].p_filesz;
 			}
 			kmem_free(kernel_map, tmp, (vm_size_t)biggest);
 			printf("PANDORA PAS: %s: read back %llu bytes, %llu differ%s\n", name,
